---
// Page 4 of the deck: the Master Plan spread, laid out as a drifting
// composition of four plates that react to the pointer on desktop.
//
// ⚠️ TWO DIFFERENT BUILDINGS EXIST IN THE SUPPLIED MATERIAL. Read this before
// adding an image here.
//
// The deck (p.4) shows a SINGLE-STOREY, L-shaped pavilion with a broad hipped
// roof — `mikasa-photo-05/06/07/08`. **That is the scheme this site shows.**
//
// The Realize Co. drawings dated 2026.03.31–04.03 and the high-resolution
// renders `01.jpg` / `02.jpg` show a DIFFERENT, two-storey house (7.763 m to
// ridge, 379.88 m² total floor area). Those are deliberately NOT built — see
// the note in scripts/optimize-images.mjs. Do not mix the two sets: they are
// not the same house, and a buyer shown both would reasonably be misled.
//
// ⚠ RESOLUTION — this is what shapes the layout below.
//
// The three interior renders are only 678 px wide originals. A click-to-enlarge
// gallery was built here first and removed for exactly that reason: putting a
// 678 px file on a full-width stage displayed it at ~1330 px, and a 2x upscale
// of a render looks like a 2x upscale of a render.
//
// So nothing in this section enlarges anything. Every plate is sized at or
// below its native width and the composition earns its interest from
// arrangement and movement instead. If better exports of this scheme arrive,
// drop them into src/assets/images/ under the same mikasa-photo-06/07/08 names
// and re-run `npm run images`; only then is there any reason to show them
// bigger.
//
// The deck presents these as finished photography, but they are renders of a
// house that does not exist on a plot being sold as bare land. Left
// unqualified that is a misrepresentation a buyer could rely on, so
// `t.concept.note` says plainly that the imagery is conceptual and that no
// building is included. Keep that note whatever else changes here.
import Figure from './Figure.astro';
import { useTranslations, type Locale } from '../i18n';

interface Props {
    locale: Locale;
}

const { locale } = Astro.props;
const t = useTranslations(locale);

// `cell` is the lg placement inside a 12-column grid; `depth` is how far the
// plate travels under the pointer, relative to its siblings. Both are per-plate
// because the composition is asymmetric on purpose — see the note on the grid.
//
// A ZIGZAG CASCADE, and the plates genuinely overlap.
//
// Reading down: aerial left, exterior right, bathroom left, bedroom right —
// each pulled up by a negative margin so it sits ON the corner of the one
// before it. Consecutive plates deliberately SHARE COLUMN 7 (1-7 / 7-11 / 3-7 /
// 7-11); that shared band is where the overlap happens, and it is what makes
// the group read as a stack of prints on a table rather than as four pictures
// in a grid. Change a col-start without checking its neighbour and the
// overlap quietly disappears — the plates go back to sitting side by side.
//
// The overlap is a CORNER CLIP, not a collision: ~80px across and only 16-24px
// down, which is barely one plate crossing the next. Deeper versions were tried
// three times and every one buried too much of the picture underneath — past
// this the plates stop reading as a cascade and start reading as a pile, and
// you lose the very images the section exists to show. The vertical figure is
// the one to move if this is ever retuned; the horizontal one is fixed by the
// shared column and should be left alone.
//
// The interiors stay at span 5 and no wider. That is the resolution ceiling,
// not a compositional choice: 5/12 of the 1920 shell is ~715px against a
// 678px original, which is the most these files take before softening.
//
// `z` ascends with the cascade so each new plate lands on top of the last, and
// each carries a shadow so the seam is legible as one plate over another.
//
// `depth` is pointer-parallax strength. The aerial is the anchor and moves
// least; the smaller plates travel further around it, which is what separates
// them into layers. `side` places the floating caption on the plate's outer
// edge — away from the overlap, so it is never buried by the next plate down.
//
// Below lg none of the cell classes apply: the four stack in source order and
// nothing overlaps, floats or moves.
const plates = [
    {
        src: '/images/villa-aerial.jpg',
        alt: t.concept.images.aerial,
        label: t.concept.plates.aerial,
        tone: 'forest' as const,
        width: 1658,
        height: 1176,
        cell: 'sm:col-span-3 lg:col-span-7 lg:col-start-1 lg:row-start-1 lg:z-10',
        depth: '0.5',
        index: '01',
        side: 'left' as const,
        note: t.concept.body,
        noteCell: 'lg:col-start-9 lg:col-span-4 lg:row-start-1 lg:self-center',
    },
    {
        src: '/images/villa-exterior.jpg',
        alt: t.concept.images.exterior,
        label: t.concept.plates.exterior,
        tone: 'forest' as const,
        width: 678,
        height: 452,
        cell: 'lg:col-span-5 lg:col-start-7 lg:row-start-2 lg:-mt-6 lg:z-20',
        depth: '1.35',
        index: '02',
        side: 'right' as const,
        note: t.concept.notes.exterior,
        noteCell: 'lg:col-start-2 lg:col-span-4 lg:row-start-2 lg:self-center',
    },
    {
        src: '/images/villa-bathroom.jpg',
        alt: t.concept.images.bathroom,
        label: t.concept.plates.bathroom,
        tone: 'interior' as const,
        width: 678,
        height: 452,
        cell: 'lg:col-span-5 lg:col-start-3 lg:row-start-3 lg:-mt-4 lg:z-30',
        depth: '1.05',
        index: '03',
        side: 'left' as const,
        note: t.concept.notes.bathroom,
        noteCell: 'lg:col-start-9 lg:col-span-4 lg:row-start-3 lg:self-center',
    },
    {
        src: '/images/villa-bedroom.jpg',
        alt: t.concept.images.bedroom,
        label: t.concept.plates.bedroom,
        tone: 'interior' as const,
        width: 678,
        height: 452,
        cell: 'lg:col-span-5 lg:col-start-7 lg:row-start-4 lg:-mt-6 lg:z-40',
        depth: '1.5',
        index: '04',
        side: 'right' as const,
        note: t.concept.notes.bedroom,
        noteCell: 'lg:col-start-2 lg:col-span-4 lg:row-start-4 lg:self-center',
    },
];---

<section id="concept" data-nav-dark class="bg-forest-deep py-24 md:py-32 lg:py-40">
    <div data-float-scene class="mx-auto max-w-shell px-6 md:px-10 lg:px-14">
        {/* The heading stands alone now. The body paragraph that used to sit
            beside it has moved down into the cascade, alongside the aerial —
            it is the one piece of copy that describes what the pictures show,
            so it belongs next to them rather than above them. That also gives
            the aerial's empty right-hand side something to hold, instead of a
            second short caption competing with the plate's own label. */}
        <div data-float data-float-depth="0.18">
            <div data-animate class="mb-8 h-px w-14 bg-brass" aria-hidden="true"></div>
            <p data-animate class="eyebrow text-brass">{t.concept.eyebrow}</p>
            <h2 data-lines class="display mt-6 max-w-[16ch] text-bone text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.4vw,2.9rem)]">
                <span class="line-mask"><span>{t.concept.heading}</span></span>
            </h2>
        </div>

        {/* The plates: a zigzag cascade that overlaps and drifts.

            ⚠️ THE SIZES HERE ARE A RESOLUTION DECISION, NOT A LAYOUT ONE.
            The three interior renders are 678px originals. A click-to-enlarge
            gallery was built here first and removed: it displayed those 678px
            files at ~1330px, and a 2x upscale of a render looks like one. So
            nothing here enlarges anything — on a 1920 shell the interiors land
            at ~700px and the aerial (1658px native) at ~1030px, every plate
            scaled DOWN and every one sharp. If these column spans grow, the
            softness comes straight back.

            Movement is deliberately FLAT — translation only, no 3D tilt. A
            rotated plate has to fake its own perspective, and against a hard
            rectangular crop that reads as a card trick rather than as depth.
            Sliding layers at different rates gives the same volume and stays
            honest to the flat, printed feel the rest of the page has.

            Three things move, all of them small:
              · pointer   each plate slides toward the cursor, by its `depth`
              · scroll    a slow vertical drift, again scaled by `depth`
              · breath    a very slow zoom on the image inside its frame

            The frames themselves never scale, only the images inside them, so
            the composition never shifts and the overlaps stay put. See
            initFloat in motion.ts. Desktop and real pointers only. */}
        <div
            class="mt-16 grid grid-cols-1 gap-5 sm:grid-cols-3 md:mt-20 lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-x-8 lg:gap-y-0"
        >
            {plates.map((plate, i) => (
                <>
                {/* The line of copy that sits opposite its plate.

                    The cascade leaves a real hole on the side each plate is
                    NOT on, and filling it with a single sentence is what turns
                    that from leftover space into a spread. Placement is
                    explicit per plate (`noteCell`) rather than automatic,
                    because the hole is a different shape at every step.

                    It floats a little further than its picture (depth 1.6 vs
                    ~0.5-1.5) so the words drift against the image rather than
                    with it, and it fades up on scroll like every other block on
                    the site. Desktop only — below lg there is no hole to fill
                    and the plates are a plain stacked column. */}
                <p
                    data-float
                    data-float-depth="1.6"
                    data-animate
                    data-animate-delay={String(0.15 + i * 0.08)}
                    class:list={[
                        'hidden leading-[1.9] lg:block',
                        // The aerial's slot carries the section's body copy, so
                        // it is set as body text. The other three are single
                        // atmospheric lines and sit quieter and smaller.
                        i === 0
                            ? 'max-w-[46ch] text-[0.95rem] text-bone/70'
                            : 'max-w-[34ch] text-[0.95rem] text-bone/55',
                        plate.noteCell,
                    ]}
                >
                    {plate.note}
                </p>

                <div
                    data-float
                    data-float-depth={plate.depth}
                    class:list={['group relative', plate.cell]}
                >
                    <div class="relative">
                        {/* The gold mount — a SEPARATE LAYER, not padding.

                            It is its own absolutely-positioned rectangle sitting
                            4px proud of the picture on every side, rather than
                            padding on a wrapper. Two reasons, and the second is
                            the important one:

                            1. A hairline is all this wants to be. Padding at
                               this width starts to read as a chunky mat; a thin
                               edge reads as a mounted print, and where two
                               plates overlap it is the line that separates them
                               — image-on-image is mush, image-on-gold-on-image
                               has an unmistakable stacking order.

                            2. Being a separate element, it can animate on its
                               OWN clock. It carries the same curtain wipe as the
                               picture with `data-reveal-delay`, so on scroll-in
                               the photograph opens first and the gold edge
                               follows a beat behind it. The frame arriving after
                               its contents is what gives the reveal depth; both
                               moving together just looks like one flat card.

                            The shadow lives here too, so the mounted object
                            casts one shadow instead of the picture casting one
                            inside its own frame.

                            lg only, like the rest of this layout. */}
                        {/* It does NOT drift independently, deliberately.

                            Giving the mount its own float depth was tried — the
                            gold sliding a few pixels against its picture, so it
                            read as a separate sheet. It looked wrong: the frame
                            slid out from under the photograph and the gold
                            vanished down one edge at the extremes.

                            It also made a rendering problem worse. The gold is
                            a rectangle 1.5px larger than the picture, so what
                            you see is a 1.5px margin. Put that on a transform
                            and it lands on fractional device pixels, where a
                            sliver that thin can round away to nothing on one
                            side and double on the other — which is the flicker
                            that showed up at 1px. Static, it renders evenly.

                            1.5px is the setting: 1px was too fragile against
                            that rounding, 2px started reading as a mat rather
                            than a hairline.

                            What it DOES keep is its own reveal clock — the
                            wipe below runs a fifth of a second behind the
                            picture's, so the photograph opens and the gold edge
                            closes in after it. That is the layered feel, done
                            with timing instead of position. */}
                        <div
                            data-reveal-img
                            data-reveal-delay="0.22"
                            aria-hidden="true"
                            class="pointer-events-none absolute -inset-[1.5px] hidden bg-brass shadow-[0_40px_90px_-40px_rgba(0,0,0,0.85)] lg:block"
                        >
                        </div>

                        {/* overflow-hidden is what turns this into a window: the
                            image inside it breathes without the frame moving. */}
                        <div class="relative overflow-hidden">
                            <Figure
                                src={plate.src}
                                alt={plate.alt}
                                width={plate.width}
                                height={plate.height}
                                tone={plate.tone}
                                loading={i === 0 ? 'eager' : 'lazy'}
                                class="w-full"
                                imgClass="h-full w-full object-cover"
                                data-reveal-img
                                data-float-zoom
                                data-animate-delay={String(i * 0.1)}
                            />

                            {/* NO nested `photo` sentinel here any more.

                                There used to be one over the aerial, flipping
                                the header from this section's gold to bone for
                                the few hundred pixels the picture occupies. It
                                was right on measurement — gold over this plate's
                                sun-rays reads about 1.2:1 — and wrong to look
                                at: the bar changed colour mid-section and then
                                changed back, which registered as a glitch
                                rather than as a response to anything.

                                The section is one gold zone now. That is the
                                same trade already made over the hero, for the
                                same reason: a bar that holds one colour through
                                a section is worth more here than a bar that is
                                individually optimal over every object it
                                crosses. If AA over this plate is ever required,
                                the fix is a top gradient on the image — ramp to
                                #2A2F25 over ~9rem, which measured 4.95:1 — not
                                a colour flip. */}
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    {/* The floating caption.

                        Hung off the plate's OUTER edge — the side away from the
                        overlap — so the next plate down never buries it, and
                        sitting just BELOW the gold mount rather than across it.
                        Straddling the mount was the first attempt and it fails
                        on contrast: bone on brass measures 2.13:1, so the label
                        half-disappears exactly where it crosses the gold. Clear
                        of the mount it sits on forest-deep at 9.4:1 and stays
                        readable along its whole length.

                        It carries a slightly higher float depth than its own
                        plate, so it drifts a little further than the picture it
                        belongs to. That small disagreement is what gives the
                        pair volume.

                        Desktop only — below lg it is display:none and the
                        plates are a plain stacked column, as before. */}
                    <figcaption
                        data-float
                        data-float-depth={String(Number(plate.depth) + 0.55)}
                        aria-hidden="true"
                        class:list={[
                            'pointer-events-none absolute z-10 hidden items-baseline gap-3 lg:flex',
                            plate.side === 'left' ? 'lg:left-0 lg:-bottom-9' : 'lg:right-0 lg:-bottom-9',
                        ]}
                    >
                        <span class="font-serif text-[0.7rem] tracking-[0.3em] text-brass [text-shadow:0_2px_10px_rgb(0_0_0/0.65)]">
                            {plate.index}
                        </span>
                        <span class="font-serif text-[1.05rem] tracking-[0.16em] text-bone [text-shadow:0_2px_14px_rgb(0_0_0/0.75)]">
                            {plate.label}
                        </span>
                    </figcaption>
                </div>
                </>
            ))}
        </div>

        <p data-animate class="mt-10 max-w-[70ch] text-[0.7rem] leading-relaxed text-bone/40">
            {t.concept.note}
        </p>
    </div>
</section>
